// Source: cppreference "Constraints and concepts" (a type-constraint must name
// a concept) and "Abbreviated function templates"; `void f(widget auto x)` is
// ill-formed in Cpp1. functions.md: `_ is C` declares a type constraint
// (e.g., a concept). Upstream emits `widget<CPP2_TYPEOF(x)>` and leaves the
// error to the Cpp1 compiler.
// expect-error: 'widget' is not a concept, so it cannot constrain '_'
widget: type = { }
f: (x: _ is widget) = { }
main: () = { }
